Encryption

Byte layouts and derivations for packets addressed to a single or a
group destination. For what the layers are for, see upstream
docs/source/understanding.rst#understanding-paths.

Vectors: test/encrypted/, test/group/

See doc/packet for the header. Only the payload is described here.


Constants

    TOKEN_OVERHEAD      48 bytes   RNS/Cryptography/Token.py#TOKEN_OVERHEAD
    DERIVED_KEY_LENGTH  64 bytes   RNS/Identity.py#DERIVED_KEY_LENGTH
    PKCS7 blocksize     16 bytes   RNS/Cryptography/PKCS7.py#BLOCKSIZE


Payload

    offset  size  field
    0       32    ephemeral X25519 public key
    32      16    initialisation vector
    48      -     ciphertext, a multiple of 16 bytes
    -32     32    HMAC-SHA256

Everything from offset 32 onward is a token in the sense below. The
minimum payload is therefore 32 + 48 = 80 bytes, which is an ephemeral
key and a token whose ciphertext is empty. Nothing the reference sends
is that short, because PKCS7 adds a full block to a plaintext that is
already block aligned and a whole block to an empty one, so 80 is a
bound of the layout rather than a length anything writes.

    RNS/Identity.py#encrypt

A shorter payload is rejected as short-payload with 80 beside it;
test/encrypted/short-payload is one byte under. test/encrypted/empty-token
is the payload at exactly 80, and it is 80 by having no ciphertext at
all; see "Padding the writer does not write". A group packet carries
no ephemeral key, so its threshold is the token overhead alone;
test/group/short-payload is one byte under that.

This is a rule of the layout above and not a check the reference makes
at that length. Identity.decrypt refuses a payload of 32 bytes or fewer
and tries anything longer, and a token that is not one fails its hmac,
so the reference drops such a packet without naming a length. The
corpus names the rule because a rejection that names nothing cannot be
told from a different rejection.

    RNS/Identity.py#KEYSIZE, RNS/Cryptography/Token.py#Cannot


Key agreement

The sender draws a fresh X25519 key for every packet and transmits its
public half. The shared secret is

    shared = X25519(ephemeral_private, recipient_public)

where recipient_public is the X25519 half of the destination identity,
or a ratchet public key when one is known for that destination.

    RNS/Identity.py#shared_key, RNS/Destination.py#selected_ratchet


Contributory behaviour, and the backend in the pin

X25519 against a point of small order yields an all-zero shared secret.
Neither end contributed to it, and every reader of the announce can
compute it, because the salt of the derivation is the recipient's
identity hash and that is public.

The two curve implementations python-rns ships do different things
with it.

    openssl backend     raises, produces no secret   the pin
    internal fallback   returns the 32 zero bytes    RNS/Cryptography/X25519.py

    RNS/Cryptography/Provider.py#PROVIDER_PYCA

Neither checks for it deliberately. RFC 7748 section 6.1 leaves the
choice to the protocol, and Reticulum makes none, so the behaviour is
whichever library is installed. See doc/identity for the same split on
the signature side, and for why the backend is part of the pin.

test/encrypted/low-order-ephemeral carries a packet whose ephemeral key
is the all-zero point. It decodes: the header is a header and the token
is laid out, because those bytes are in the packet. Everything that
needs the agreement is "-".

    shared_key         -
    signing_key        -
    encryption_key     -
    hmac_valid         -
    plaintext_length   -
    plaintext          -

That is a third value for hmac_valid beside yes and no, and it means
the question was never reached rather than answered. The same
distinction plaintext_length already carries.

An implementation that returns the zero secret rather than refusing
will report hmac_valid no here, not "-", and the vector will say so.

test/linkdata/low-order-request is the same six lines one layer up. On
a link no packet carries an ephemeral key, so the point that costs the
agreement is in the link request instead, and it arrives one packet
earlier. See doc/link, section "Key derivation".


Key derivation

    derived = HKDF-SHA256(shared, salt, context, 64)

    salt      the recipient's identity hash, 16 bytes
    context   empty
    length    64 bytes

HKDF is RFC 5869 with SHA-256. The salt is the identity hash of the
recipient, derived from its own public key as in doc/identity. It stays
the identity hash even when the shared secret came from a ratchet: the
ratchet changes the agreement, not the salt.

    RNS/Identity.py#derived_key, RNS/Identity.py#get_salt,
    RNS/Cryptography/HKDF.py#hkdf


Token

The 64 derived bytes are split, signing key first:

    offset  size  field
    0       32    HMAC key
    32      32    AES key

A 64-byte key selects AES-256-CBC. Token also accepts a 32-byte key,
which selects AES-128-CBC and halves both parts. Identity encryption
never produces one and neither does Token.generate_key, so the only way
to reach that mode is to configure a group destination with a 32-byte
key. test/group/aes128 is that destination, and the only vector in the
corpus that is not AES-256.

    RNS/Cryptography/Token.py#_signing_key

Verification comes before decryption. The HMAC covers the
initialisation vector and the ciphertext, not the ephemeral key:

    hmac = HMAC-SHA256(signing_key, iv || ciphertext)

A token whose HMAC does not match is rejected without decrypting.

    RNS/Cryptography/Token.py#verify_hmac,
    RNS/Cryptography/Token.py#decrypt

Reticulum calls this construction a token because it is Fernet with the
version byte and the eight-byte timestamp removed. An implementation
reaching for a Fernet library will produce nine bytes too many at the
front.

    RNS/Cryptography/Token.py#Token


Padding

PKCS7 with a block size of 16. The final byte gives the padding length;
a value above the block size is invalid.

    RNS/Cryptography/PKCS7.py#bs

That is the only bound applied. A length of zero removes nothing and
yields the whole final block, and the padding bytes themselves are
never compared against the length. A decoder that adds either check
refuses tokens the reference opens.

The bound is read from a byte the ciphertext has to hold, so a token
with no ciphertext at all reaches it with nothing to read: unpad
indexes the last byte of an empty block and the packet is dropped
there, one step past the hmac it passed.

    RNS/Cryptography/PKCS7.py#unpad


Padding the writer does not write

pad writes 1 to 16 bytes and reaches neither end of what unpad accepts,
so four tokens in the corpus were written with pad replaced by the
identity for the one call that packed them. The plaintext handed to
Token.encrypt is then the padded block itself, and every other byte is
the reference's: its key, its initialisation vector, its cipher, its
hmac. Each of the four names the replacement in its own meta, and the
verdict each records is the reference reading back what it wrote.

    test/group/zero-padding          length 0, which removes nothing
    test/encrypted/empty-token       a token of 48 bytes, no ciphertext
    test/encrypted/long-padding      length 17, one past the bound
    test/encrypted/loose-padding     length 3, on bytes that disagree

The first two are the two ends of what unpad accepts, and the reference
opens one of them: zero removes nothing and yields the whole block, and
an empty ciphertext has no byte to read the length from. The third is
the one length unpad refuses, and it is refused for being 17 and not
for what the other 15 bytes hold. The fourth is the check unpad does
not make: three bytes are removed on the word of the last of them,
while the two above it say 0xaa and 0xbb.

A padding length above the block size and a padding length no writer
wrote are the two halves of the same disagreement, and an
implementation is free to be stricter than the reference only in the
direction that drops packets nobody sends. loose-padding is the one
that costs: a receiver checking the padding bytes drops a packet the
reference delivers 13 bytes of.

A decoder that pads its own way meets all four in the read direction
only, which is where every one of them belongs: the corpus rebuilds
their raw from the iv, the ciphertext and the hmac in expect and never
from the plaintext, so no encoder here pads anything.

    RNS/Cryptography/PKCS7.py#pad, RNS/Cryptography/Token.py#encrypt

A fifth token is not on that list and cannot join it. Replacing pad
reaches as far as the cipher and no further: Token.encrypt hands the
block to AES-CBC, which takes a multiple of 16 bytes or nothing. Both
backends refuse, the pinned one with a ValueError and the internal one
with an assertion, so unlike the curve there is no split to record.

    RNS/Cryptography/AES.py#AES_256_CBC

So a ciphertext of 15, 17 or 33 bytes has no vector. A reader that
decrypts one is outside what the corpus measures, and the four above
say nothing about it: their bound is the padding length, and this one
is the cipher's.


Vector format

kind: encrypted, determinism: encode

raw is three lines:

    line 1    the recipient private key, 64 bytes
    line 2    the ratchet private key, 32 bytes, or "-"
    line 3    the complete packet

The private keys are in the vector because a packet cannot be decoded
without them. They are the upstream test keys and a ratchet generated
for the purpose; neither guards anything.

expect opens with the two keys, as recipient_private and
ratchet_private, so that it holds every byte of raw and the packet can
be rebuilt from it. It then carries the header fields, the payload
decomposition, and every intermediate value down to the plaintext:
shared_key, signing_key and encryption_key are all printed. An
implementation whose decryption fails can find the step where it
diverged rather than guessing.

    plaintext_length   the length in bytes, or "-" if nothing was decrypted
    plaintext          the bytes as hex, or "-" when empty or absent

The two together are unambiguous: length 0 with plaintext "-" is an
empty plaintext, length "-" is a failure.

The three key fields and hmac_valid are "-" when no shared secret
exists at all. See the section on contributory behaviour above.

Encode class. The ephemeral key and the initialisation vector are drawn
at random, so no implementation can produce this packet a second time;
expect records both as they came out, so it can lay this one back out.
That is what cmd/check tests. It is also why the corpus carries no
vector that asks an implementation to encrypt: the reference ships a
token decryption vector and no token encryption vector for the same
reason.


Group destinations

Destination type 1. The payload is a bare token:

    offset  size  field
    0       16    initialisation vector
    16      -     ciphertext, a multiple of 16 bytes
    -32     32    HMAC-SHA256

    RNS/Destination.py#GROUP

There is no ephemeral key, because there is no key agreement, and no
HKDF, because there is nothing to derive from. The key is configuration
shared out of band, and the two halves of the token are the first and
second halves of it as configured.

    signing_key    = group_key[0:n]
    encryption_key = group_key[n:2n]

That is the whole of what makes this type different: the same three
fields, keyed by a value that went through no function.

n is half the key, and the key is 32 bytes or 64. This is the one place
either length turns up, because it is the one key a human configures:
test/group/aes128 against every other vector of the kind. See the
section above on the two key lengths. The hmac is 32 bytes at both, so
the token overhead does not move and no length rule changes.

An outbound group destination still needs an identity, and its hash is
derived exactly as a single destination's is. The identity plays no
part in the encryption.

    RNS/Destination.py#GROUP

Vectors: test/group/minimal and test/group/altered-hmac.

kind: group, determinism: encode

raw is two lines:

    line 1    the group key, 64 bytes
    line 2    the complete packet

expect opens with the key, then the header fields, the three parts of
the token, the two halves of the key, and the plaintext. There is no
shared_key field: nothing was agreed.
